=C2=A0 -&gt; The reason for last is to defend against class of attacks incl=
uding<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0Triple Handshake.<br>
- The &quot;application master secret&quot; needs to be CR function of:<br>
=C2=A0 1) handshake master secret.<br>
=C2=A0 2) Static Diffie-Hellman input.<br>
=C2=A0 -&gt; The hash is not needed here (but can be included).<br>
=C2=A0 -&gt; Can be different in both directions (which has some benefits,<=
br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0in this case, the hash should be included).<br>
- The &quot;exporter master secret&quot; needs to be CR function of:<br>
=C2=A0 1) hanshake master secret.<br>
=C2=A0 2) Static Diffie-Hellman input.<br>
=C2=A0 3) The hash of *Hello, *Keyshare EncryptedExtensions and whatever is=
<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0carrying the server static DH key packets (more can be =
included<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0for a good measure).<br>
=C2=A0 -&gt; The reason why EncryptedExtensions needs to be included is in =
case<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0it contains some extensions affecting TLS-Extractor. Th=
e reason<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0server static DH key is needed is again to guard agains=
t attacks<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0like THS.<br>
- The &quot;resume premaster secret&quot; needs to be CR function of:<br>
=C2=A0 1) hanshake master secret.<br>
=C2=A0 2) Static Diffie-Hellman input.<br>
=C2=A0 3) Hash of entiere handshake.<br>
=C2=A0 -&gt; The reason why last needs needs to be entiere handshake is tha=
t<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0otherwise session may be resumed in inocnsistent state.=
<br>
=C2=A0 -&gt; Also so that resumption doesn&#39;t destroy forward security.<=
br>
- Online signature (if present) TBS needs to include:<br>
=C2=A0 1) *Random<br>
=C2=A0 2) Purpose of signature (e.g. server PoP)<br>
=C2=A0 3) Ciphersuite #<br>
=C2=A0 4) Prf-hash of messages so far<br>
=C2=A0 -&gt; *Random is to support privsep. Which is extremely useful even =
with<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0pure SW if you have MMU available (and most of the time=
 you do).<br>
=C2=A0 -&gt; Ciphersuite # is to pin the prf-hash algorithm (I think that i=
s<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0just the simplest way).<br>
<br>
<br>
The &quot;function of&quot; listings above are what I consider the absolute=
<br>
minimum, that&#39;s the reason why &quot;application master secret&quot; ha=
sh input<br>
is not marked as needed. It can (and should) be included.<br>
<br>
Also, one should take care that none of the different secrets may<br>
end up being same (except by very bad luck).<br>
<br>
The only thing above -05 fails is &quot;exporter master secret&quot;, which=
<br>
is the same as handshake master secret nor does it hash enough state.<br>
<br>
<br>
Also, other comments:<br>
- I consider HKDF-HMAC-SHA256 to be too weak.<br>
=C2=A0 -&gt; I think it needs at least HMAC-SHA384 (also would just give on=
e<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0prf-hash).<br>
=C2=A0 -&gt; SHA384 is FASTER than SHA256 on modern hardware (that&#39;s wh=
y<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0SHA-512/256 exists). But SHA3-384 is slower than SHA3-2=
56.<br>
- There is no need to intermingle static DH key with signature, even if<br>
=C2=A0 statid DH key exists.<br>
=C2=A0 -&gt; Signatures work like in -05.<br>
=C2=A0 -&gt; Can completely eliminate signature if one has fixed-DH cert (D=
HF<br>
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0is WAY simpler than signature primitive).<br>
<br>
<br>
Also, will *server* 0RTT be supported, even with client authentication?<br>
It would set the following boundary conditions:<br>
- Server application keys can&#39;t include hashes of client certificate,<b=
r>
=C2=A0 as those need to be computable immediately on server finished.<br>
- The same for exporter master secret.<br>
<br>
The restrictions here are:<br>
- Replay protection is provoded.<br>
- There is no authentication (the server hasn&#39;t heard client&#39;s<br>
=C2=A0 certificate, which keeps misuse potential way down).<br>
<br>
Usecases:<br>
- Protocol banners, feature negoitiation (ALPN doesn&#39;t scale!),<br>
=C2=A0 etc...<br>
<br>
<br>
Also regarding splitting application keys to directional ones:<br>
- Allows JIT derivation, eliminating temporarily invalid keys.<br>
- Allows rekeying (since requirement is no dead time and arbitrarily<br>
=C2=A0 delayed responses, as breaking this causes problems for all<br>
=C2=A0 applications).<br>
